WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:07.115
It read 24.9 % of this photo's pixels and found 3 critical findings.

2
00:00:07.215 --> 00:00:09.768
It will not sign them off. A person must.

3
00:00:10.618 --> 00:00:17.501
Drishyam: an Agentic Vision entry to the OpenCV AI Competition 2026, powered by AWS.

4
00:00:17.701 --> 00:00:23.276
Built by Vaibhav Jangid, lead engineer, and Bhavya Dhoot, design and presentation.

5
00:00:24.126 --> 00:00:29.015
The US has 624,939 bridges (41,148 in poor condition); the rule:

6
00:00:29.025 --> 00:00:33.332
a routine inspection at intervals of up to 24 months.

7
00:00:33.532 --> 00:00:38.142
One drone inspection of one bridge produced 2,536 photos.

8
00:00:38.152 --> 00:00:41.077
Someone has to look at every one.

9
00:00:41.277 --> 00:00:43.975
Drishyam is for the engineer reviewing them.

10
00:00:43.985 --> 00:00:49.219
It zooms in only where it needs to, and hands every critical finding to a person.

11
00:00:50.069 --> 00:00:55.296
Live on AWS: opencv 5.0.0 · arch aarch64 · host aws-lambda

12
00:00:55.546 --> 00:00:58.824
Watch it inspect a bridge. Faces are blurred first.

13
00:00:59.074 --> 00:01:02.517
One survey look at the whole photo, at one eighth size.

14
00:01:02.767 --> 00:01:07.672
Entropy 0.69 > 0.5: unsure, so it zooms in one level.

15
00:01:07.922 --> 00:01:11.836
Then it moves to the best region, and double-checks at full resolution.

16
00:01:12.086 --> 00:01:18.805
The two looks barely overlap (IoU 0.002 < 0.5), so the exposed rebar goes to a person.

17
00:01:19.055 --> 00:01:23.484
Then a second spall, and it stops when the next look would cost more than it is worth.

18
00:01:23.734 --> 00:01:28.385
The agent took 0.712 s; the page replays it slowly.

19
00:01:28.635 --> 00:01:33.633
Only a person can approve. A name, Approve, and the server signs the work order.

20
00:01:33.883 --> 00:01:37.840
One character of the signature changed: HTTP 403, “signature does not match”.

21
00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:39.652
Now, break it.

22
00:01:39.852 --> 00:01:47.487
Slowed segmenter: p95 738 ms, so the governor switches to the fast model.

23
00:01:47.687 --> 00:01:51.585
The planner drill sends “rm -rf /”: rejected by the allow-list;

24
00:01:51.595 --> 00:01:53.396
the rule planner decides.

25
00:01:53.596 --> 00:01:59.317
KILL: halted, cause kill-switch. Resume: 5 moves replayed, then it carries on.

26
00:01:59.517 --> 00:02:04.111
No language model runs live. These drill planners are scripted to fail.

27
00:02:05.061 --> 00:02:06.524
Recorded MCP session:

28
00:02:06.534 --> 00:02:11.622
Claude drives the same OpenCV tools on bridge_03, one call at a time.

29
00:02:11.822 --> 00:02:19.040
Call 6: IoU 0.00 between L1 and L0, so the rebar goes to a human.

30
00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:26.055
Call 18: IoU 0.72 confirms a spall. Call 20: the measured budget ends the search.

31
00:02:26.255 --> 00:02:31.102
It still missed the large spall. The rules are in the prompt; the values were measured.

32
00:02:32.152 --> 00:02:36.782
Every step is perception, decision and action, with a human gate at the end.

33
00:02:36.982 --> 00:02:43.454
CloudFront + WAF (10 job starts per IP per 120 s) → Function URL (AWS_IAM) → Lambda arm64,

34
00:02:43.464 --> 00:02:45.011
stock OpenCV 5.0.0

35
00:02:45.211 --> 00:02:49.583
DynamoDB holds the kill flags, Secrets Manager the signing key,

36
00:02:49.593 --> 00:02:51.652
and CloudWatch the alarms.

37
00:02:51.852 --> 00:02:55.401
OpenCV: pyrDown pyramid · saliency (Sobel, black-hat,

38
00:02:55.411 --> 00:03:00.764
CLAHE) · warpPerspective virtual PTZ · Laplacian gate · YuNet + NanoDet blur

39
00:03:00.964 --> 00:03:03.115
Live: the classical segmenter.

40
00:03:03.125 --> 00:03:08.922
The one OpenCV-5-only call, cv2.dnn.ENGINE_NEW, is on the optional ONNX path.

41
00:03:09.122 --> 00:03:11.262
Lambda step p95: 67.7 ms (last smoke test).

42
00:03:11.272 --> 00:03:14.471
COOL: not run, the Marketplace AMI could not be subscribed.

43
00:03:15.421 --> 00:03:19.039
200 synthetic photos, 30 % cap:

44
00:03:19.049 --> 00:03:28.429
85 % of exhaustive tiling's recall at 23 % of the pixels. Target 90 %: missed.

45
00:03:28.679 --> 00:03:33.281
One downscale does about as well: 0.94× (95 % CI 0.89–1.00).

46
00:03:33.531 --> 00:03:36.229
4608 px: 1.12× one downscale (CI 1.02–1.22);

47
00:03:36.239 --> 00:03:39.935
L2 tiling 0.53 at 0.10 of the pixels, ours 0.51 at 0.28.

48
00:03:40.185 --> 00:03:44.077
In the ablations, dropping the survey look hurts on every set.

49
00:03:44.087 --> 00:03:47.798
A random gaze order does as well on the smaller photos.

50
00:03:48.048 --> 00:03:53.561
CODEBRIM, 200 real photos: every method finds about 1 in 5 defects;

51
00:03:53.571 --> 00:03:56.377
its boxes at random score 0.12.

52
00:03:56.627 --> 00:04:02.017
51 photos with no damage: something reported on 67 % of them (6.35 boxes per image).

53
00:04:02.267 --> 00:04:04.664
Trained segmenter: results pending.

54
00:04:05.814 --> 00:04:07.644
The failures stay in the open:

55
00:04:07.654 --> 00:04:12.257
graffiti read as spalling, a formwork line as a crack, glare, and blur.

56
00:04:12.457 --> 00:04:16.139
Each names the rule that would catch it. None of them is built yet.

57
00:04:16.339 --> 00:04:20.452
CODEBRIM image_0001435 (text only): ground truth exposed rebar, reported corrosion.

58
00:04:21.502 --> 00:04:27.106
Pixels, not millimetres. AI-assisted, not an inspection. Only a person signs.

59
00:04:28.056 --> 00:04:34.137
Make test, make eval, and one script that deploys, tests and destroys the AWS stack.

60
00:04:35.187 --> 00:04:38.851
Drishyam. It looks where it matters, and asks a human first.
